  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 AM EDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast:\n\nBroad mid/upper level troughing across the Great Lakes early this \nmorning, part of rather aggressive northern stream flow on north \nside of elongated southern Conus upper level ridge/heat dome. \nStalled baroclinic axis well to our south across the lower Ohio \nValley...the eastern extent of an active thermal gradient extending \nfrom deep low pressure vicinity James Bay. Large area of slow east \nmoving high pressure dominating our surface conditions, bringing \nwith it partly cloudy to mostly clear skies and dropping \ntemperatures to more normal levels in the 50s to lower 60s.\n\nNorthern Conus flow regime will continue to remain quite progressive \nthrough this weekend right into next week. This will bring a fairly \ntypical early fall weather pattern to northern Michigan, with dry \nweather interrupted at times by passing waves and their attendant \nshowers. \n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns:\n\nTemperature trends and addressing those occasional shower concerns \nthrough the entirety of the forecast period.\n\nDetails: \n\nNot much too talk about today through Friday as high pressure \ncontinues to remain in control, bringing with it mostly sunny/clear \nskies. Despite this sun, temperatures today will cool to more normal \nmid-September readings in the upper 60s to middle 70s. Should have \nabout ideal conditions for a good nocturnal temperature response \ntonight with those clear skies and winds that go light to calm. \nEasily expect most of our colder interior locations to dip down into \nthe 40s. Wouldn't be terribly surprised to see some of our ice-box \nlocations within the Au Sable River valley make a run into the upper \n30s. Simple airmass modification and developing southwest return \nflow under those still mostly sunny skies will help temperatures \nrecover quickly on Friday, with highs well back up into the 70s.\n\nStrong shortwave trough passing well to our north looks to drag our \nnext cold front through the area Saturday. Best forcing along this \nfront passes well to our north, and deepest tropical moisture feed \nremains tied up with that Ohio Valley baroclinic axis. Expect a few \nprogressive moving showers to fire across our area on Saturday (best \nchance likely remaining north of the Big Bridge), but definitely not \nexpecting anything significant.\n\nAt least some evidence our weather may turn a bit more active later \nSunday into early next work week as next wave rotates up through the \nnorthern Lakes into southern Canada. Nice axis of Pacific moisture \nexpected to accompany this wave, with more Gulf of America moisture \ninflux as well as deep southerly flow propels north into the region. \nSystem could be a decent rain-maker for some areas across the \nregion, with initial moisture-rich isentropic upglide yielding to \nrather impressive cold frontal dynamics. Just something to monitor \nas we head into early next week.\n\nExact temperature trends this weekend into next week will be highly \ndependent on those cloud and shower trends. With that said, not \nseeing any evidence of any significant bouts of excessive warmth or \ncold...expecting temperatures to fall well within the range of what \nis considered normal heading through the middle of September.",
